sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Among the 256 elementary (two-color, nearest-neighbor) cellular automata started from a single black cell, about two-thirds produce patterns that stay of fixed size and about one-third produce patterns that grow forever, with about 14% yielding patterns more complicated than repetition.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Most elementary cellular automata give fixed-size patterns from a single black cell";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “More Cellular Automata” (from p. 53), p. 57. Scope: the 256 elementary cellular automata, single-black-cell initial condition"
}
